generated_answer:
Short answer
- Use CSS `scroll-behavior: smooth;` as the simplest, lowest-effort approach (apply globally or to a specific scrollable container).
- Add a small JS fallback / enhancement for controlled scrolling (e.g., `scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth' })`) when you need focus management, older-browser fallbacks, or custom easing.
- Always respect accessibility: handle focus after scrolling and honor users who prefer reduced motion.

Semantic HTML5 note (per your preference to always include this): put the navigation links in a <nav> inside <header>, target sections inside <main> using <section id="...">, and ensure elements you scroll to are focusable (or temporarily made focusable with tabindex="-1") so screen readers get context.

Examples

1) Simple CSS-only global solution (works for anchor links)
```css
/* put in your main stylesheet */
html {
  scroll-behavior: smooth;
}

/* respect user preference for reduced motion */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  html { scroll-behavior: auto; }
}
```

HTML (semantic)
```html
<header>
  <nav class="navbar navbar-expand-lg">
    <div class="container-fluid">
      <a class="navbar-brand" href="#">Portfolio</a>
      <ul class="navbar-nav">
        <li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="#about">About</a></li>
        <li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="#skills">Skills</a></li>
        <li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="#projects">Projects</a></li>
        <li class="nav-item"><a class="nav-link" href="#contact">Contact</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </nav>
</header>

<main id="main-content">
  <section id="about">...</section>
  <section id="skills">...</section>
  <section id="projects">...</section>
  <section id="contact">...</section>
</main>
```
- With this in place, clicking any `<a href="#section-id">` will smoothly scroll to that section in modern browsers.

2) JavaScript enhancement (recommended when you need to set focus for accessibility or for a scroll-to-top button)
```javascript
// Smooth scroll helper that falls back gracefully
function smoothScrollTo(target) {
  if (!target) return;

  // prefer built-in smooth behavior if supported
  if ('scrollBehavior' in document.documentElement.style) {
    target.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
  } else {
    // simple fallback (instant) for very old browsers
    const top = target.getBoundingClientRect().top + window.pageYOffset;
    window.scrollTo(0, top);
  }

  // Accessibility: ensure the target is focused and announced by screen readers.
  // If the element is not normally focusable, give it temporary tabindex -1.
  const hadTabindex = target.hasAttribute('tabindex');
  const prevTab = target.getAttribute('tabindex');
  if (!hadTabindex) target.setAttribute('tabindex', '-1');

  target.focus({ preventScroll: true }); // preventScroll because we already scrolled
  // Optionally remove temporary tabindex after a short delay
  if (!hadTabindex) {
    setTimeout(() => {
      target.removeAttribute('tabindex');
    }, 1000);
  }
}

// Wire nav links with delegation to handle focus+history properly
document.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  const a = e.target.closest('a[href^="#"]');
  if (!a) return;

  const href = a.getAttribute('href');
  if (href === '#' || href === '#!') return;

  const target = document.querySelector(href);
  if (target) {
    e.preventDefault();
    smoothScrollTo(target);

    // update URL without jumping (so browser back/forward works)
    history.replaceState(null, '', href);
  }
});

// Example: scroll-to-top button
document.getElementById('scroll-to-top')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
  // smoothScrollTo(document.documentElement); // scroll to top element
  window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' }); // works in modern browsers
});
```

Accessibility & best practices
- Focus management: after scrolling to target, call .focus() so keyboard & screen-reader users are placed at the right spot (use temporary tabindex="-1" if necessary).
- Reduced motion: respect `prefers-reduced-motion` (do not animate if user prefers reduced motion).
- Semantic structure: use <nav> for navigation, <main> for page content, <section> with headings for each target, and ensure the scrolled-to targets have meaningful headings for screen readers.
- History management: update the URL with history.replaceState or pushState if you want the anchor reflected without triggering another jump.
- Performance: CSS `scroll-behavior` is cheap; JS is okay for a few handlers. Avoid heavy per-frame JS during scrolling.
- Browser support: CSS `scroll-behavior` is supported in modern browsers; JS fallback ensures compatibility.

Which approach to pick?
- Use CSS `scroll-behavior: smooth;` for the simplest, cleanest implementation for anchor links.
- Add the JS enhancement when you need:
  - to autofocus scrolled-to elements for accessibility, or
  - to support older browsers, or
  - to control scroll timing/easing beyond the default.
- Combine both: CSS for the majority; JS only where you need precise behavior.

Small checklist to verify implementation
- [ ] Add `html { scroll-behavior: smooth; }` and the reduced-motion media query.
- [ ] Ensure navigation is semantic: <nav> within <header>, links point to section IDs inside <main>.
